@synopsis
**yume** **--server** *host* **-i** *identity_composite* [ *mode* ] [ *options*
]
**yume** **--config** *path* [ *mode* ]
**yume** **completion** **bash**
**yume** **export** *file.yss* [ **--password-stdin** ]
**yume** **import** *file.yss* [ **--password-stdin** ]
**yume** **--help**
**yume** **--version**
@end

## DESCRIPTION

**yume** is the YUME client. It opens an authenticated carrier to man:yumed(8)
and sends local application streams through that carrier. The mandatory 2.0
carrier remains HTTP/2 for the full connection and carries masked WebSocket
binary messages after a Chrome-shaped page/asset prime and RFC 8441 extended
CONNECT. Its mandatory inner channel combines ephemeral ML-KEM-1024, X25519,
and a random file PSK with salted HKDF, then uses one-use AES-256-GCM message
keys and independent directional epochs. AUTH v2 verifies the composite Ed25519
and ML-DSA-87 client identity and binds authentication and the key schedule to
the live TLS exporter. The client authenticates the server through TLS trust,
optional leaf pinning, and optional operator proof.

The product version is 0.3.0-dev1; these commands speak transport-v2 wire
0.2.0-dev6. The experimental YTP/1 replacement and its schema-1 tools are
separate. A schema-1 configuration does not drive this client.

The client can expose a local SOCKS5 proxy, run one command through the tunnel,
create local or reverse forwards, attach to relay clients, or use the directory
and chat/file control features when the server permits them.

## ROUTING MODEL

The common direct route is:

@diagram direct_route

The client network normally sees an HTTPS-looking connection to the YUME
server. The target normally sees the YUME server's egress IP. The server
process receives target metadata because it must open the outbound target
socket unless the application itself routes through another layer such as Tor.

### Client through Tor to a hidden-service yumed

With **--tor** or **--proxy socks5://host:port** the client routes the YUME
carrier through a SOCKS5 proxy (typically the local Tor daemon). The
destination hostname is sent as ATYP_DOMAIN so **.onion** targets resolve on
the proxy side; no DNS leaks. On the server, **yumed** binds 127.0.0.1 and Tor
publishes a HiddenService that forwards onion traffic to it.

@diagram tor_hidden_service

### Server-side Tor egress

@diagram server_tor_egress

This makes the target see a Tor exit instead of the YUME server, but the
**yumed** process still receives the requested target unless the application
itself is only asking the server to connect to a Tor entry.

### Tor over YUME

@diagram tor_over_yume

For target anonymity from the YUME server, prefer this route. The YUME server
usually sees only a Tor guard or bridge, not the final website.

### YUME over Tor

@diagram yume_over_tor

This hides the client's real IP from the YUME server, but it is slower and the
Tor exit can see that a connection is being made to the YUME server.

### Tor -> YUME -> Tor

@diagram tor_yume_tor

This can reduce linkability for passive observers because neither edge sees
both the real client and the final target. It is also much slower and does not
hide the requested target from **yumed** unless the application itself uses Tor
over YUME.

### Common routes summary

@code text
Fast path:
  app -> yume ==YUME==> yumed -> target

Server-side Tor egress:
  app -> yume ==YUME==> yumed -> server Tor ...> target

Tor over YUME:
  Tor Browser -> local Tor -> yume ==YUME==> yumed -> Tor entry ...> target

YUME over Tor:
  app -> yume -> client Tor ...> yumed -> target

Tor -> YUME -> Tor:
  app -> yume -> client Tor ...> yumed -> server Tor ...> target

VPN/proxy after YUME:
  app -> yume ==YUME==> yumed -> VPN/proxy -> target
@end

## TRANSPORT LAYERS

### Carrier frame shape

@code text
+----------------------------------------------------------------------+
|  OUTER TLS 1.3 CARRIER                                               |
|                                                                      |
|  YUME frame header                                                   |
|    type, flags, stream id                                            |
|                                                                      |
|  INNER PAYLOAD                                                       |
|    encrypted with BaseFWX / AES-256-GCM                              |
|    authenticated with frame context                                  |
+----------------------------------------------------------------------+
@end

Inner encryption protects YUME frame payloads between the client and server. It
does not replace application HTTPS and does not hide target metadata from a
server that is asked to open the target directly.

### HTTP/2 obfs sequence

@code text
+--------------------------------+
|  TLS HANDSHAKE                 |
|  real TLS 1.3                  |
+--------------------------------+
        |
        v
+--------------------------------+
|  HTTP/2 PREFACE                |
|  PRI * HTTP/2.0                |
+--------------------------------+
        |
        v
+--------------------------------+
|  PROJECT H2 SETTINGS           |
|  SETTINGS + WINDOW_UPDATE      |
+--------------------------------+
        |
        v
+--------------------------------+
|  OBFUSCATED REQUEST            |
|  POST /token/nonce HEADERS     |
+--------------------------------+
        |
        v
+--------------------------------+
|  YUME FRAMES                   |
|  carrier data begins           |
+--------------------------------+
@end

The obfs path uses a standards-conformant HTTP/2 opening before the YUME
carrier begins. Its settings and headers are project templates, not
byte-identical output from a named browser, and the later carrier is not a
full-session HTTP/2 tunnel.

### Stream multiplexing

@code text
+----------------------------------------------------------------------+
|  ONE AUTHENTICATED CARRIER                                           |
|                                                                      |
|  stream 1  OPEN ifconfig.me:80  -> DATA -> DATA <- CLOSE             |
|  stream 2  OPEN 1.1.1.1:443   -> app TLS bytes <-> target bytes      |
|  stream 3  OPEN api.site:443  -> another independent connection      |
+----------------------------------------------------------------------+
@end

## SHARE FILES

@options
@opt **export** *file.yss*
@cli spell: export <file>
@cli help: Encrypt the current config + auth key
@cli help: + CA cert + PQ pubkey into a
@cli help: password-protected .yss file ("yume
@cli help: secure store"). Prompts for a
@cli help: password (12+ chars, twice for
@cli help: confirmation). Up to 16 MiB; created
@cli help: 0600 without following or overwriting.

Encrypt the merged client configuration, composite identity, and selected trust
material into a password-protected YUME secure-store file. The destination is
created exclusively with mode 0600; existing paths, symlinks, and non-regular
entries are refused. Output is capped at 16 MiB and a partial publication is
removed on failure.
@opt **import** *file.yss*
@cli spell: import <file>
@cli help: Decrypt a .yss file and write the
@cli help: extracted keys + a ready-to-use
@cli help: config.json to ~/.yume/imported/
@cli help: <server-host>/. Prints the exact
@cli help: `yume --config <path>` to use them.
@cli help: Input is capped at 16 MiB before KDF.

Read and decrypt a YUME secure-store file, then publish its extracted private
files and ready-to-use configuration beneath the imported-profile directory.
Input is limited to 16 MiB before parsing or password-KDF work. Plaintext,
password, and bundled secret buffers owned by YUME are wiped on success and
failure, including retained capacity. Export guards partial JSON secret values
before later allocations. This is best-effort cleanup; it does not guarantee
erasure of JSON library scratch, caller copies, swap, or core dumps.
@opt **--password-stdin**
@cli spell: --password-stdin
@cli help: For export/import, read the password from stdin;
@cli help: for import, also skip the interactive write
@cli help: confirmation

Read the share password from standard input for non-interactive export or
import. Avoid command-line password arguments, which can be visible in process
listings.
@end

## MODES

@options
@opt **--socks** *[addr:]port*
@cli spell: --socks [addr:]port
@cli help: Start a SOCKS5 proxy
@cli help: Example: --socks 127.0.0.1:1080
@cli help: Without addr the bind is 0.0.0.0, every IPv4
@cli help: interface and not only loopback.

Start a local SOCKS5 proxy. The optional *addr* must be an IP literal, for
example **127.0.0.1:1080**, **0.0.0.0:1080**, or **[::1]:1080**. Without an
address YUME keeps the historical wildcard bind. Use **socks5h://** in clients
that support it so DNS is resolved through YUME.
@opt **-L** *[bind:]lport:host:port*
@cli spell: -L [bind:]lport:host:port
@cli help: Local forward

Open an SSH-style local forward. Local connections to *lport* are relayed
through YUME to *host*:*port*. The optional *bind* must be an IP literal.
@opt **-R** *[bind:]rport:host:port*
@cli spell: -R [bind:]rport:host:port
@cli help: Reverse forward

Open a reverse forward. The server listens on *rport* and relays accepted
connections back to the client's *host*:*port*. The optional *bind* must be an
IP literal on the server side.
@opt **--lport** *port***, --rhost** *host***, --rport** *port*
@cli spell: --lport <port> --rhost <host> --rport <port>
@cli help: Long-form local forward (all three required)

Long-form spelling of a local forward. Local connections to *lport* are relayed
through YUME to *host*:*port*. All three must be given together. **-L** is the
compact spelling and also accepts a bind address. Without one, the listener
keeps the historical wildcard bind.
@opt **--run** , **-c** *command*
@cli spell: --run <cmd>
@cli help: Run a command through Yume

Run a local command with its TCP/UDP sockets routed through YUME.
@opt **--proxycmd**
@cli flags: --dest --dport
@cli spell: --proxycmd --dest <host> --dport <port>
@cli help: One-shot SOCKS client for a proxy hook

Act as a one-shot SOCKS5 client for another program's proxy hook. YUME connects
to a SOCKS listener on 127.0.0.1 at the **--socks** port, or 1080 when
**--socks** is absent, issues a CONNECT for **--dest** and **--dport**, then
relays standard input and output over that stream. This is the form **--run**
generates as a man:ssh(1) ProxyCommand. It is not supported on Windows.
@opt **--dest** *host*

Destination host for **--proxycmd**.
@opt **--dport** *port*

Destination port for **--proxycmd**.
@opt **--exec** *command*
@cli spell: --exec <cmd>
@cli help: Controlled-client EXEC request; currently denied

Send an outbound EXEC request for *command* through the controlled-client mode
selected by **--control**. The request syntax is retained, but the current
server answers it with an explicit safety denial, so no command runs. The value
is refused if it begins with **-**.
@opt **--allow-exec**
@cli complete: no
@cli spell: --allow-exec
@cli help: Unavailable; inbound execution stays disabled

Rejected. Inbound remote command execution is disabled until child processes
have bounded shutdown support, so passing this option fails argument parsing.
@opt **--directory**
@cli spell: --directory
@cli help: List visible relay endpoints

List visible relay endpoints from the authenticated server.
@opt **--chat** *id-or-name*
@cli spell: --chat <id|name>
@cli help: Start chat

Start a chat with a relay endpoint when allowed by policy.
@opt **--send-file** *id-or-name* *path*
@cli spell: --send-file <id|name> <path>
@cli help: Send a file

Send a file through the relay channel when allowed by policy.
@opt **--send-bytes** *id-or-name* *path*
@cli spell: --send-bytes <id|name> <path>
@cli help: Send raw bytes

Send raw bytes through the relay channel when allowed by policy.
@opt **--admin-attach** *id-or-name*
@cli spell: --admin-attach <id|name>
@cli help: Open trusted admin channel

Open a trusted admin channel to a relay endpoint when both sides permit it.
@opt **--control** *[id]*
@cli spell: --control [id]
@cli help: Control a registered client

Attach to a controlled client.
@opt **--id** *id*
@cli spell: --id <id>
@cli help: Controlled-client identifier for --control

Controlled-client identifier for **--control**.
@opt **--list-controlled**
@cli spell: --list-controlled
@cli help: List controlled clients

List the controlled clients registered with the server.
@opt **--attach-local**
@cli spell: --attach-local
@cli help: Attach to a local yume

Attach to a local running YUME instance.
@opt **--packet-tun** *ifname*
@cli spell: --packet-tun <ifname>
@cli help: Attach an operator-created Linux TUN (no route changes)

Attach packet-bulk mode to an operator-created Linux TUN interface. YUME does
not create routes, NAT rules, or the interface, and this exclusive data-plane
mode cannot be combined with SOCKS, forwards, relay, benchmark, or control
modes.
@opt **--monero-rpc**
@cli spell: --monero-rpc
@cli help: Start the built-in Monero RPC application
@cli help: codec on 127.0.0.1:18089. Wallets can
@cli help: use --daemon-address 127.0.0.1:18089.

Start the built-in Monero RPC application codec and listen on
**127.0.0.1:18089**. A wallet reaches it with **--daemon-address
127.0.0.1:18089**. Application codecs are protocol-aware local service shims,
not raw TCP forwards. The server must also permit the codec for this key.
@opt **--monero-rpc-listen** *addr:port*
@cli spell: --monero-rpc-listen <addr:port>
@cli help: Select the Monero codec and its loopback listener

Start the Monero RPC codec on an explicit listener. Equivalent to
**--monero-rpc** together with **--codec-listen**.
@opt **--codec** *name*
@cli values: monero-rpc
@cli spell: --codec monero-rpc
@cli help: Same codec mode using the generic codec flag

Select an application codec by registry name. In the transport-v2 runtime the
only built-in codec is **monero-rpc-v1**, which is also accepted as
**monero-rpc** or **monero**.
@opt **--codec-listen** *addr:port*
@cli spell: --codec-listen <addr:port>
@cli help: Override codec listener; must be loopback
@cli help: (default 127.0.0.1:18089).

Override the codec listener. The address must be a loopback IP literal and the
port must be 1-65535. The default is **127.0.0.1:18089**.
The JSON **app_codec_listen** field must also parse successfully when this
option overrides it. A malformed endpoint refuses the load without publishing
partial configuration.
@opt **--quick-bench**
@cli spell: --quick-bench
@cli help: Run the local device benchmark smoke profile;
@cli help: no server, auth key, or config required.

Run the bounded local device benchmark smoke profile.
@opt **--full-bench**
@cli spell: --full-bench
@cli help: Run the local YUME 2.0 transport benchmark;
@cli help: reports MiB/s, no server/config required.

Run the extended local device benchmark profile. These local profiles are not
WAN, stealth, classifier, or release-qualification evidence.
@end

## BENCHMARK OPTIONS

Two independent benchmark families exist. The endpoint benchmark runs against a
live server started with **yumed --bench**. The local device profiles
**--quick-bench** and **--full-bench** are listed under MODES. They need no
server, identity, or configuration, and they require a binary built with the
selftest harness. Neither family is WAN, stealth, classifier, or
release-qualification evidence.

@options
@opt **--bench**
@cli spell: --bench
@cli help: Run authenticated endpoint up/down benchmark
@cli help: against yumed --bench, then exit.

Run the authenticated endpoint upload/download benchmark against the server,
then exit. This also selects non-interactive output.
@opt **--bench-full**
@cli spell: --bench-full
@cli help: Longer real-server profile; defaults to
@cli help: 1024 MiB per direction and 64 streams.

Run the longer endpoint profile. Unless overridden it uses 1024 MiB per
direction and 64 concurrent streams.
@opt **--bench-mib** *n*
@cli spell: --bench-mib <N>
@cli help: Benchmark payload per direction (default 256).

Benchmark payload per direction in MiB. Non-positive values select the default
of 256; values above 16384 are clamped to 16384.
@opt **--bench-chunk-kib** *n*
@cli spell: --bench-chunk-kib <N>
@cli help: DATA chunk size (default: SOCKS/relay buffer, 64 KiB).

Upload DATA chunk size in KiB. The default matches the SOCKS/relay read buffer,
currently 64 KiB. Non-positive values select that default; values above 256 are
clamped to 256. Only the default chunk size reports a production stream shape.
@opt **--bench-streams** *n*
@cli spell: --bench-streams <N>
@cli help: Concurrent benchmark streams (default 1, max 240).

Concurrent benchmark streams. Default **1**, clamped to **1**-**240**.
@opt **--bench-direction** *direction*
@cli spell: --bench-direction <D>
@cli help: both, up, or down (default both).

Measured direction: **both**, **up**, or **down**. Default **both**.

The remaining options tune the local device benchmark and are forwarded to it.
They have no effect without **--quick-bench** or **--full-bench**.
@opt **--configs** *list*
@cli spell: --configs <list|all>
@cli help: Local benchmark config subset

Comma-separated config subset, or **all**. The default runs the direct baseline
and the mandatory yume-v2 stack.
@opt **--list-configs**
@cli spell: --list-configs
@cli help: Print local benchmark configs and exit

Print the local benchmark config names and exit.
@opt **--duration-sec** *n*
@cli spell: --duration-sec <N>
@cli help: Full-mode duration hint (30..600)

Full-mode target duration hint in seconds. Range **30**-**600**, default
**180**. It scales the payload size.
@opt **--latency-iters** *n*
@cli spell: --latency-iters <N>
@cli help: Local benchmark echo iterations

Echo round trips per config. Default 120. Full mode uses 360.
@opt **--bulk-mib** *n*
@cli spell: --bulk-mib <N>
@cli help: Local benchmark bulk size

Bulk echo size per config in MiB. Quick mode defaults to 32. Full mode computes
a duration/config-count value and clamps it to 512-8192.
@opt **--streams** *n*
@cli spell: --streams <N>
@cli help: Local benchmark concurrent streams

Concurrent bulk streams per config. Quick mode defaults to 1; full mode uses
64.
@opt **--client-threads** *n*
@cli spell: --client-threads <N>
@cli help: Local benchmark client threads (0 = auto)

Client I/O threads. Use **0** for automatic selection.
@opt **--server-threads** *n*
@cli spell: --server-threads <N>
@cli help: Local benchmark server threads

Server I/O threads. Quick mode defaults to 2. Full mode defaults to one quarter
of the detected logical CPUs, clamped to 4-16.
@opt **--cooldown-ms** *n*
@cli spell: --cooldown-ms <N>
@cli help: Pause between local benchmark configs

Pause between configs for fairer sweeps. Quick mode defaults to 500; full mode
uses 1000.
@opt **--repeat** *n*
@cli spell: --repeat <N>
@cli help: Trials per local benchmark config

Run each config *n* times and report the median-throughput trial. Quick mode
defaults to 1; full mode uses 3.
@opt **--one-way**
@cli spell: --one-way
@cli help: Measure local upload with sink/ack, not echo

Measure one-way upload with sink and acknowledgement instead of echo. Quick
mode defaults to echo; full mode enables one-way measurement.
@opt **--json** *path*
@cli spell: --json <path>
@cli help: Write local benchmark JSON

Write the JSON result file to *path*.
@opt **--json-stdout**
@cli spell: --json-stdout
@cli help: Print local benchmark JSON after the table

Print the JSON result to standard output after the table.
@opt **--dev**
@cli spell: --dev
@cli help: With --quick-bench/--full-bench, show raw
@cli help: component tables and phase timings.

Show raw component tables, phase timings, and row details.
@opt **--color, --no-color**
@cli spell: --no-color
@cli help: Disable colored benchmark grades.
@cli spell: --color
@cli help: Force colored benchmark grades.

Force or disable ANSI colors in benchmark output.
@opt **--keep-workdir**
@cli spell: --keep-workdir
@cli help: Keep local benchmark logs and generated keys

Keep the temporary logs and generated keys the run creates.
@end

## CONNECTION OPTIONS

@options
@opt **--server** *host*
@cli spell: --server <host>
@cli help: Server address

Server hostname or IP address.
@opt **--port** *port*

Server port. If omitted, the client uses the configured/default server port.
@opt **--cluster** *host[:port]*
@cli spell: --cluster <spec>
@cli help: Cluster entry-point short form:
@cli help:   host                    (port 443)
@cli help:   host:port
@cli help:   [ipv6]:port
@cli help: Sets --server + --port together.
@cli continuation: 27

Cluster entry-point short form for **--server** and **--port**. Bracket IPv6
literals as **[2001:db8::1]:443**.
@opt **--proxy** *socks5://[user[:pass]@]host:port*
@cli spell: --proxy socks5://[user[:pass]@]host:port
@cli help: Route the connection to the Yume server
@cli help: through a SOCKS5 proxy. The hostname is
@cli help: resolved on the proxy side, so .onion
@cli help: targets work through Tor.
@cli continuation: 27

Reach the YUME server through a SOCKS5 proxy. The proxy resolves the
destination name, so an **.onion** address reaches a hidden service rather than
failing to resolve locally.
@opt **--tor**
@cli spell: --tor
@cli help: Shorthand for --proxy socks5://127.0.0.1:9050

Shorthand for **--proxy socks5://127.0.0.1:9050**, the address the Tor daemon
listens on by default.
@opt **--no-proxy**
@cli spell: --no-proxy
@cli help: Override config; connect directly

Ignore any outbound proxy carried by the configuration and dial the server
directly.
@opt **-i, --auth** *path*
@cli file: yes
@cli spell: -i, --auth <path>
@cli help: Identity key (composite Ed25519+ML-DSA-87)

Composite Ed25519 + ML-DSA-87 identity used to authenticate this client to the
server.
@opt **--secondary-auth** *path*
@cli file: yes
@cli spell: --secondary-auth <path>
@cli help: Repeat once for every data-only SOCKS tunnel
@cli help: after the primary. Requires exactly N-1 values
@cli help: with --tunnels N; all must authenticate.
@cli indent: 6

Composite identity for one data-only secondary SOCKS tunnel. Repeat exactly
*N-1* times with **--tunnels** *N***.** Every secondary must authenticate
before the SOCKS listener is considered ready; the primary admin factor is
never replayed on these data-only tunnels.
@opt **--admin-auth** *path*
@cli file: yes
@cli spell: --admin-auth <path>
@cli help: Second key for an admin session. Must differ from
@cli help: --auth and be enrolled in the server admin store.
@cli help: Presenting it is the claim; there is no admin flag.
@cli indent: 6
@cli column: 29

Distinct composite second identity for an admin session. It must be enrolled in
the server's admin store; presenting this key is the admin claim.
@opt **--config** *path*
@cli file: yes
@cli spell: --config <path>
@cli help: Config file

Read client configuration from a JSON file.
@opt **--save-server**
@cli spell: --save-server
@cli help: Save the effective merged config to --config
@cli help: Include cleared values and automatic/disabled settings
@cli column: 26

Write the effective merged client configuration back to the **--config** file
before connecting, creating or updating that file.
Automatic thread/control-port selection, one tunnel, disabled SOCKS/shaping,
and cleared paths or trust references replace their previous saved values.
The native writer emits **tls_pin** and the combined **app_codec_listen**
endpoint. Serialization failure preserves the existing file.
@opt **--threads** *n*
@cli spell: --threads <n>
@cli help: IO threads (0 = auto)

I/O thread count. Use **0** for automatic selection.
@opt **--rekey-window** *n*
@cli spell: --rekey-window <n>
@cli help: Concurrent directional epoch offers (1..64;
@cli help: default 8). Each prepared epoch adds
@cli help: one negotiated epoch budget per rekey
@cli help: round trip. Capped by the server.

Concurrent directional epoch offers this client accepts, and, capped by the
server's advertised depth, the number it keeps prepared for sending. Range
**1**-**64**, default **8**. Each prepared epoch adds one negotiated epoch byte
budget per rekey round trip on high-latency links. Under the default Extreme
mode that budget is 256 KiB, with independent limits of 512 frames and 500 ms
sender-active time. The JSON configuration key **security_mode** selects
Extreme, Normal, Soft, or a complete bounded Ultimate policy; see
**docs/SECURITY_MODES.md**.
@opt **--udp**
@cli spell: --udp
@cli help: Enable UDP forwarding

Enable UDP forwarding.
@opt **--tcp**
@cli spell: --tcp
@cli help: Force TCP only

Force TCP-only behavior.
@opt **--allow-local-ip**
@cli spell: --allow-local-ip
@cli help: Allow private and loopback destinations

Allow private, loopback, and link-local destinations where policy permits.
@opt **--run-ipv4**
@cli spell: --run-ipv4
@cli help: Prefer IPv4 for --run

Prefer IPv4 behavior for **--run**.
@opt **--root**
@cli spell: --root
@cli help: Keep root privileges

Keep root privileges instead of dropping them where supported.
@end

## SECURITY OPTIONS

@options
@opt **--obfs-secret-file** *path*
@cli file: yes
@cli spell: --obfs-secret-file <p>
@cli help: 32-byte admission secret as exactly 64
@cli help: lowercase hex characters in a protected file
@cli column: 26

Load the mandatory admission secret. The file must contain exactly 64 lowercase
hexadecimal characters encoding 32 random bytes, with no newline and no
group/world permissions.
@opt **--inner-psk-file** *path*
@cli file: yes
@cli spell: --inner-psk-file <p>
@cli help: Mandatory 32-byte inner PSK in the same format
@cli column: 26

Load the separate mandatory inner PSK in the same strict file format. It is
mixed by salted HKDF; Argon2 is not used. Ratchet modes never disable the
mandatory hybrid exchange, HKDF, or per-message AES-256-GCM keys.
@opt **--require-operator-identity**
@cli spell: --require-operator-identity
@cli help: Require proof authorized by the selected operator CA

Require proof that the endpoint is authorized by the selected operator CA. This
authenticates the operator; it does not prove no logging or anonymity.
@opt **--operator-ca-cert** *path*
@cli file: yes
@cli spell: --operator-ca-cert <path>
@cli help: Operator CA certificate used to verify the host

Operator CA certificate used to verify endpoint authorization.
@opt **--accept-monitoring**
@cli spell: --accept-monitoring
@cli help: Pre-accept the privacy warning when proof is absent

Answer the privacy-risk consent prompt in advance for a server that presents no
operator identity proof. An ordinary non-interactive connection must set this
or require an authorized operator, otherwise the connection is refused. The
endpoint benchmark is the deliberate prompt-free exception. On the CLI,
**--require-operator-identity** takes precedence if both flags are supplied;
the stricter facade JSON contract rejects that redundant combination.
Consenting records an acknowledgement. It does not reduce what the operator can
observe.
@opt **--tls-ca** *path*
@cli file: yes
@cli spell: --tls-ca <path>
@cli help: TLS CA certificate

TLS CA certificate.
@opt **--tls-pin** *sha256*
@cli spell: --tls-pin <sha256>
@cli help: Pin TLS certificate fingerprint

Pin the server TLS certificate fingerprint.
@end

## TLS STEALTH OPTIONS

@options
@opt **--profile** *name*
@cli values: chrome
@cli spell: --profile <name>
@cli help: chrome (the only 2.0 fixture)

Select the pinned Chrome 151 Linux profile. In the transport-v2 runtime,
**chrome** is the only accepted value.
@opt **--transport-profile** *id*
@cli spell: --transport-profile <id>
@cli help: chrome151-node24-v1 (exact dev6 identity)

Select the authenticated dev6 transport identity. The only accepted value is
**chrome151-node24-v1**.
@opt **--tls-backend** *name*
@cli spell: --tls-backend <name>
@cli help: openssl-chrome151 (default), chrome151 helper,
@cli help: or openssl-diagnostic
@cli continuation: 27

Select **openssl-chrome151** for the default in-process, patched-OpenSSL
emitter, **chrome151** for the optional packaged Linux helper, or
**openssl-diagnostic** for the explicit non-Chrome-parity diagnostic path.
There is no silent fallback.
@opt **--tls-helper** *path*
@cli file: yes
@cli spell: --tls-helper <path>
@cli help: Helper path; valid only with chrome151 backend

Use an explicit absolute helper path when **--tls-backend chrome151** is
selected. Other backends reject this option. The launcher requires a regular
executable owned by root or the current user and rejects group/world-writable
files.
@opt **--tls-name** *host*
@cli spell: --tls-name <host>
@cli help: SNI/certificate/HTTP Host name when
@cli help: --server is an IP or alternate route
@cli column: 26
@cli continuation: 27

Set the TLS SNI, certificate-verification name, and HTTP Host identity
independently from the dial address. This is required when connecting by IP to
a certificate issued for a DNS name.
@opt **--hide-in-the-crowd chrome**
@cli spell: --hide-in-the-crowd chrome
@cli help: Explicit spelling for the pinned Chrome 151
@cli help: HTTP profile; other profiles are rejected.

Explicit spelling for the same pinned Chrome 151 HTTP User-Agent profile. Other
HTTP profiles are rejected by the transport-v2 runtime.
@opt **--tls-fingerprint-log**
@cli spell: --tls-fingerprint-log
@cli help: Diagnostic-backend fingerprint metrics

Log TLS fingerprint metrics for the explicit **openssl-diagnostic** backend.
Other backends reject this diagnostic-only option.
@opt **--tls-fingerprint-log-path** *path*
@cli file: yes
@cli spell: --tls-fingerprint-log-path <path>
@cli help: Diagnostic-backend fingerprint log path

Path for TLS fingerprint log output.
@opt **--tls-fingerprint-verify**
@cli spell: --tls-fingerprint-verify
@cli help: Diagnostic-backend endpoint verification

Verify fingerprints against a test endpoint for the explicit
**openssl-diagnostic** backend. Chrome backends use the emitted-byte wire gates
instead.
@opt **--tls-fingerprint-test-endpoint** *host*
@cli spell: --tls-fingerprint-test-endpoint <host>
@cli help: Test endpoint host

Host used for fingerprint verification tests.
@opt **--self-dpi**
@cli spell: --self-dpi
@cli help: Warn when local carrier metadata stops
@cli help: matching the selected disguise profile

Warn when local carrier metadata stops matching the selected disguise profile.
The checks are warning-only observations of this client's own traffic and are
not a filtering-resistance claim.
@opt **--no-self-dpi**
@cli spell: --no-self-dpi
@cli help: Disable self-DPI warnings

Disable the self-DPI warnings.
@opt **--outer-carrier-evidence** *absolute-path*
@cli file: yes
@cli spell: --outer-carrier-evidence <absolute-path>
@cli help: Write one mode-0600 live H2/WebSocket behavior
@cli help: report outside Git. Requires the pinned Chrome
@cli help: backend and exact 1-MiB one-shot benchmark.

Write the exact-policy one-flow outer-carrier evidence bundle. This option is
admitted only for the documented pinned Chrome 151 H2 benchmark geometry and
does not by itself prove browser parity, classifier resistance, or release
qualification. See **TRANSPORT_PROFILES.md**.
