## INTERACTIVE CONSOLE

When a session stays attached, the console accepts commands such as:

@code text
    help, status, directory, contacts, forget, invites, chat, send,
    send-file, send-bytes, accept, reject, history, history-delete,
    admin attach, admin status, admin sessions, admin stop, whoami, quit
@end

**contacts** lists every peer whose relay identity this client trusts, merged
with the current directory so each row shows presence as well as where its
trust came from (**tofu**, **explicit**, or **configured**). Trust is durable
local state and the directory is a live overlay, so contacts still list when
the server is unreachable; presence then reads **unknown**.

**forget** *endpoint-id* removes a trust-on-first-use pin. It refuses a
fingerprint named by the configuration or one carrying a durable explicit
marker: those record an out-of-band authorization decision, which is reversed
by changing the configuration rather than from the console.

## EXAMPLES

Start a local SOCKS5 proxy:

@code text
yume --server origin.example.net -i ~/.yume/id_ed25519 --socks 127.0.0.1:1080
@end

Use curl with remote DNS through the proxy:

@code text
curl -x socks5h://127.0.0.1:1080 https://ifconfig.me
@end

Run one command through YUME:

@code text
yume --server origin.example.net -i ~/.yume/id_ed25519 --run "curl
https://1.1.1.1"
@end

Create a local forward:

@code text
yume --server origin.example.net -i ~/.yume/id_ed25519 -L 2222:127.0.0.1:22
@end

Require operator identity proof:

@code text
yume --server origin.example.net -i ~/.yume/id_ed25519 --socks 127.0.0.1:1080 \
     --require-operator-identity --operator-ca-cert /etc/yume/operator-ca.pem
@end

## SECURITY NOTES

Inner encryption protects YUME frame payloads between the client and server. It
is not browser-to-target end-to-end encryption; HTTPS in the application still
provides that layer.

The YUME server needs enough target metadata to open outbound sockets. If the
server must not learn final targets, run Tor locally and route Tor through
YUME.

Use deployment-specific PQ key files.

Operator identity proof verifies a CA-authorized endpoint and the associated
mode asks the server to minimize identifying logs. It cannot prove that the
operator follows that policy, and does not remove hosting logs, payment
records, DNS records, browser fingerprints, logged-in accounts, or
time-correlation risks.

## FILES

@options
@opt *config/yume.json*

Example local client configuration. Its keys form a closed set: an unknown or
retired key is a load error, never a silently ignored setting.
@opt *~/.yume/id_ed25519*

Common client identity key location.
@opt */etc/yume/*

Common system configuration directory.
@opt */usr/share/doc/yume/*

Installed documentation in packaged builds.
@end

## INSTALLATION

From a build tree:

@code text
./ezbuild.sh
sudo cmake --install build
@end

Manual man-page install:

@code text
sudo install -Dm644 docs/man/yume.1 /usr/local/share/man/man1/yume.1
sudo mandb
@end

Debian package builds produced by CPack install this page automatically.

## SEE ALSO

man:yumed(8), man:ssh(1), man:curl(1), man:tor(1)

Project documentation is installed under */usr/share/doc/yume/* when built as a
package.
